        m_Metadata:
          m_Items: []
        m_Value: Load Level
    - rid: 552786097007231050
      type: {class: StringEntry, ns: Unity.Localization, asm: UnityEngine.LocalizationRuntimeModule}
      data:
        m_KeyId: 2584642994176
        m_Metadata:
          m_Items: []
        m_Value: "Note: this will be configurable eventually.\n\nWith menu closed\n    Movement:\n        - Forward/Back/Left/Right: WASD\n        - Down/Up: Q/E\n        - Go faster: Hold Shift\n        - Forward/Back in steps: Mouse wheel (when the pointer isn't over the UI)\n\n    Looking:\n        - Move mouse while holding RMB\n\n    Panning:\n        - Drag with the middle mouse button, or hold Space and drag with the left mouse button, to grab the level and slide across it (horizontally). What you grabbed stays under the pointer: in perspective, at the height of what's under it (or, over nothing, the floor or ceiling corner nearest to where you pointed).\n\n    The camera can't leave the level's bounds.\n\n    Orthographic view (the Orthographic toggle, bottom):\n        - Up/Down/Left/Right across the view: WASD\n        - Camera down/up (what's above it isn't drawn): Q/E\n        - Zoom in/out: Mouse wheel\n        - Pan: drag with the middle mouse button, or hold Space and drag (follows the pointer exactly)\n        - Looking around is locked, with the map's up at the top of the view\n\nUI Controls:\n    - Open/Close Menu: Tab or Escape\n    - Show/Hide All UI: ` (~ key without Shift)\n\nSurface Dragging:\n    - You can drag on a surface to adjust textures in Texture mode, when the Palette mode (right side) is set to Edit.\n        - If you hold the control key while starting to drag (you can let go after the drag starts) then contiguous surfaces with the same bitmap will also align to the dragged surface.\n        - With the surface selected in this mode, you can also use arrow keys to nudge and snap it in 128th increments (classic pixel distance).\n\nObject Dragging:\n    - In Objects mode (and for sound sources in Sounds mode), with the Select tool, drag an object to move it. It moves across the level at the height it was grabbed, into whichever polygon it's over.\n        - With Snapping on (alt/option inverts it), it snaps to the nearest sixteenth of a world unit along X and Y.\n        - Moving into another polygon, it keeps its height in the world, kept within that polygon's floor and ceiling. With Snapping on (the setting itself) or while holding control, it instead keeps its distance from the polygon's floor (or ceiling, when From Ceiling is enabled).\n\nPoint Dragging:\n    - In Geometry mode, with the Select tool and Points shown (the visualization options on the left), drag a point's handle to move it. It moves across the level at the height of the corner it was grabbed by.\n        - With Snapping on (alt/option inverts it), it snaps to the nearest sixteenth of a world unit along X and Y.\n        - Its sides' textures stay where they were at the lines' other ends, and floors' and ceilings' textures stay where they were in the world. While holding control, they move with the point instead (pressing or letting go of control during the drag switches between the two, as though it had been that way for the whole drag).\n        - With Prevent Invalid Moves on (Settings), it stops short of where it would make a polygon around it invalid (concave, inside out, crossing itself, or without area), showing what's wrong where it's held back from. Hold shift while dragging to let it go there anyway (shown in red, with a marker where it last was valid).\n        - Dropping it on a neighboring point collapses the line between them: the two points become one, and a polygon left with fewer than three corners is removed.\n\nPolygon Dragging:\n    - In Geometry mode, in the Orthographic view, with the Select tool, drag a selected polygon to move it whole. Its corners move together (stretching the polygons around it that share them), and its objects and annotations move with it.\n        - With Snapping on (alt/option inverts it), how far it moves snaps to sixteenths of a world unit, so it stays as aligned to the grid as it was.\n        - Textures, Prevent Invalid Moves, and collapsing lines work as they do for points.\n\nHotkeys:\n    - Frame Selected: f (with nothing selected, it frames what was last selected, or the nearest object)\n    - Toggle X-Axis Lock: x\n    - Toggle Y-Axis Lock: y\n\nModifier Keys (must be held):\n    - Invert Snapping Setting: alt/option\n    - In Texture > Edit Mode, you can select a surface (normal click) then alt-click a surface of the same geometry type (Polygon or Side) to align that surface's texture to the selected one.  SIdes must be neighbors, but polygons (ceilings and floors) can be anywhere.  The assigned textures are not required to be the same, and will not be updated when aligning.\n    - In Texture > Edit Mode, if you hold control while beginning to drag on a surface, all contiguous surfaces with the same bitmap will also be aligned to the dragged surface.\n    - When dragging an object into another polygon, hold control to keep its distance from the polygon's floor (or ceiling) instead of its height in the world.\n    - When dragging a point, hold control to move the textures around it with the point.\n    - When dragging a point, hold shift to let it go where it would make a polygon invalid.\n    - When dragging a polygon, hold control to move the textures around it with it, and shift to let it go where it would make a polygon invalid.\n\nPainting:\n    - In Texture > Paint Mode, you can click on surfaces to assign the selected texture swatch to those surfaces."
